# The settings window's compile list: one repository-relative path per line, in the order
# csc is given them. This file is the only place that order is written down.
#
# It was written out by hand in seventeen places - the build script, the release measurement,
# and fifteen tests that compile the window to look at it - so a file added to the window was
# a file added seventeen times, and a test that missed the edit went on compiling a window
# that was not the one being shipped while still passing. `tests/guiscan.py` reads this, and
# `tests/test_guiscan.py` fails if a tracked `gui/*.cs` is neither on this list nor known to
# be built separately.
#
# Order matters to the build's reproducibility, not to the compiler: csc takes its sources in
# the order given and `build/normalize_pe.py` fixes the timestamp but not the layout, so two
# builds of one tag have to hand them over identically.
#
# `gui/McpLauncher.cs` is deliberately not here. It is its own console-subsystem executable,
# built by the same script from its own single source, because it inherits Codex's standard
# streams and hands them to the MCP server - which is the whole reason it exists.
#
# `[installer]` (v0.6.16) is the installer's window: the setup programs' wizard, the Dashboard's update and the
# uninstall Add or remove programs runs. The setup programs are compiled with it, `[controls]` and `[generated]`
# as well (build/make_setup.py), so it is written in the controls' terms and names nothing of the two halves.
# `gui/HostApp.cs` (v0.6.17) is in it for the same reason: both programs ask whether they run inside another app's
# private view of Windows, and get out of it.
#
# `gui/Brand.cs` is generated by `build/make_brand.py` from the Python palette, so the window
# and the Codex panel cannot end up different colours. It is compiled like any other source.
#
# The `[group]` lines below are read too. The window is two halves - `[settings]`, the process
# and the Settings page, and `[dashboard]`, the navigation and its five pages - drawn with
# `[controls]`, in the palette `[generated]` writes. A rule about what the window's own code may
# contain is a rule about the first three; `[generated]` is where the palette's values
# legitimately are. Twenty-odd tests named the file they meant, which is why v0.6.10-alpha's
# split had to move them all: they name a group now, and a file added to a group joins every
# rule about it without a second edit.

[settings]
gui/SettingsApp.cs
gui/WindowJson.cs
gui/SettingsPage.cs
gui/SettingsPower.cs
gui/SettingsHomes.cs
gui/SettingsOwn.cs
gui/WindowReopen.cs
gui/WindowAudit.cs
gui/SettingsRemove.cs
gui/WindowRender.cs
gui/ChildTree.cs

[dashboard]
gui/Dashboard.cs
gui/DashboardPages.cs
gui/DashboardCompat.cs
gui/DashboardData.cs
gui/DashboardActions.cs
gui/DashboardMaintenance.cs
gui/DashboardTools.cs
gui/DashboardVersions.cs

[installer]
gui/InstallerFlow.cs
gui/InstallerWindow.cs
gui/HostApp.cs

[controls]
gui/SoftTheme.cs
gui/SoftDepth.cs
gui/SoftLayout.cs
gui/SoftFields.cs
gui/SoftCombo.cs
gui/SoftList.cs
gui/SoftMenu.cs
gui/SoftCallout.cs
gui/Marks.cs

[generated]
gui/Brand.cs
